FWIW I ended up using an agent to set the socket time-out and it seems to be working well for me now.

You can demonstrate the different time-out behaviours in the node REPL

```javascript
const Axios = require('axios');
const http = require('http');
const agentWithoutTimeout = new http.Agent();
const agentWithTimeout = new http.Agent({ timeout: 2000 });
const instance1 = Axios.create({ timeout: 5000, httpAgent: agentWithoutTimeout });
const instance2 = Axios.create({ timeout: 5000, httpAgent: agentWithTimeout });
// http://www.google.com:81/ will always timeout because it drops SYN packets (see https://stackoverflow.com/a/100859/1153203)
instance1.get("http://www.google.com:81/").catch(e => { if (e.code === "ECONNABORTED" ) { console.log("instance 1 socket timed out") } else { console.log(e) } });
instance2.get("http://www.google.com:81/").catch(e => { if (e.code === "ECONNABORTED" ) { console.log("instance 2 socket timed out") } else { console.log(e) } });
 ```

You will see "instance 2 timed out" after two seconds but instance1 won't time out

The two timeouts are also independent, the agent timeout can be lower than than Axios timeout.

If the request is cancelled due to the socket timeout set on the HTTP agent, Axios will throw an error reporting that the timeout occurred with the value specified in the Axios config. For example, you'll see on the above example, if you log the error, it will report that the timeout was 5000ms when it actually timed out after 2000ms. This behaviour does seem like me to be a bug rather than just a missing feature.

