The order in transformers is deterministic as well

```js
const axios = require("axios");

const sleep = (ms) => new Promise((resolve, reject) => setTimeout(resolve, ms));

(async () => {
        const client = axios.create({
                baseURL: "https://httpbin.org",
        });

        client.interceptors.request.use(async (config) => {
                console.log('sleeping 5000')
                await sleep(5000);
                return config;
        });

        client.interceptors.request.use(async (config) => {
                console.log('sleepig 1000')
                await sleep(1000);
                return config;
        });
        client.interceptors.request.use(async (config) => {
                console.log('sleepig 2000')
                await sleep(1000);
                return config;
        });

        console.log(await client.get("/headers"));
})();
```

This yields

```
sleepig 2000
sleepig 1000
sleeping 5000
```

which means the last registered interceptor is the first one called, interceptors are a stack, while transformers are queued. 
Interceptors can be added dynamically after creation of the axios instance, making it possibly undeterministic, however, transformers are determinstic because of how they are instantiated. Transformers are also intuitive and meant to change the data and payload specifically. Interceptors on the other hand can alter the whole config dynamically, more flexible, more unituitive in their function. 

So, why not allow a choice between the two and let people choose what they may?

