> axios.defaults.headers.post['Content-Type'] = 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded';

This is already the default configuration value and this won't work for historical reasons ¯\_(ツ)_/¯. The content type must be set explicitly, otherwise, the object's payload will be encoded into a JSON object. 

The following should work as `content-type` header will be omited for GET requests:

```js
axios.defaults.headers['Content-Type'] = 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded';
```

> It's inconvenient to add headers: { "content-type": "application/x-www-form-urlencoded" } to each request.

You can create your own Axios instance with the `content-type` header preset. 
Besides, creating custom instances should be the recommended approach for making config presets. Changing the root instance configuration should be marked as an anti-pattern, as it can lead to configuration conflicts if Axios is used inside third-party modules. Unfortunately, there is a lot of users' code that depends on this behavior.

```js
  const urlEncodedAxios = axios.create({
